# The accepted residual for scripts/bandit-audit.py (issue #962).
#
# ONE LINE PER (file, rule), with the COUNT and the issue tracking it. This
# file is the mechanism that replaced codex-power-pack's inherited
# `--skip B104,B108,B310,B602`, and the difference is the whole of #962:
#
#   * a --skip turns a rule off across the whole tree, forever, invisibly.
#     Measured here, it removes 10 of this tree's 11 MEDIUM+ findings.
#   * a line below is a COUNT BASELINE for one (file, rule). A new site in the
#     same file is a finding; a new site in a fresh file is a finding; and a
#     recorded site that gets FIXED turns the gate red until its line comes
#     out, so a suppression cannot outlive the count it records.
#
# WHAT A COUNT DOES NOT ESTABLISH, said here because a reader of this file is
# the person most likely to rely on it: it is not a per-site identity. Fix one
# accepted site and add a different one in the same file, and the count is
# unchanged and the gate stays green - the REPLACEMENT is invisible. That is a
# deliberate bound, not an oversight: a line number is invalidated by every
# edit above it and a snippet hash by every reformat, and a ledger that reddens
# on unrelated edits gets switched off (ADR 0009). Whether an accepted site is
# still the same site is a question for review of the diff that moved it and
# for #1113, and `tests/test_bandit_audit.py` pins the blind spot so it stays a
# committed fact rather than a claim in a comment.
#
# Format: finding <path> <test-id> <count> <issue>
# Whole-line comments only - `#` also opens every issue reference on a record.
#
# Every `finding` line here is dispositioned by #1113.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SECOND RECORD TYPE, AND WHY IT IS NOT A SUPPRESSION (issue #1114)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Format: reviewed-low <test-id> <issue>
#
# The LOW-severity band is below this gate's threshold and gates nothing. It is
# counted and printed on every run, which keeps "below the gate" from reading as
# "not examined" - but a single total cannot say the thing a reader actually
# needs: whether anyone READ what is down there. Seven known rule classes and an
# eighth nobody has ever looked at move the same integer by one.
#
# So each LOW rule class present in this tree carries one line below, recording
# that it was read and where the reasoning lives. A LOW finding whose rule class
# has no such line is reported and reddens the gate.
#
# THIS IS THE OPPOSITE OF A `--skip`, which is worth stating in the file where
# both would live. A `--skip` REMOVES a distinction bandit could draw, tree-wide,
# forever, invisibly. A line below ADDS one this gate could not draw at all, and
# suppresses nothing - there is nothing below the threshold for it to suppress.
# Its only effect is to mark a class as read.
#
# Deliberate asymmetry with the `finding` records above: a `finding` line that
# matches nothing is STALE and reddens (a suppression outliving its finding is a
# blindfold), while a `reviewed-low` line that matches nothing is a printed NOTE
# and does not (it hid nothing, and reddening on the removal of the last
# `import subprocess` in a file is how a ledger gets switched off - ADR 0009).
#
# No count on these records, deliberately. A count is what makes a `finding`
# line a baseline that a new site breaks; a rule class is read or it is not, and
# a count here would redden on every ordinary edit that adds a `subprocess`
# call while saying nothing about whether anybody read anything.
#
# The dispositions, per class, with the evidence:
#   docs/decisions/0010-bandit-low-band-disposition.md
#
# The current counts are NOT written down here. Three hardcoded numbers for this
# band already went stale (133 in issue #1114, 136 in this header, 135 on the
# tree that fixed them); the gate prints the live count and the per-rule
# breakdown on every run, which is where a number belongs.

# B602 - subprocess with shell=True. Six sites, all in lib/cicd, all running a
# command string that arrives from project configuration (.claude/cicd.yml
# steps and skip_if conditions, .claude/deploy.yaml guardrail and smoke
# commands). The shell is the feature; the open question #1113 carries is whose
# string is allowed to reach it.
finding lib/cicd/bootstrap.py B602 1 #1113
finding lib/cicd/deploy/docker_compose.py B602 1 #1113
finding lib/cicd/deploy/guardrails.py B602 1 #1113
finding lib/cicd/smoke.py B602 1 #1113
finding lib/cicd/steps.py B602 2 #1113

# B108 - hard-coded temp path. ONE site left, and it is the one with the real
# story: the deploy lock's predictable path in a world-writable directory IS
# the cross-user mutual-exclusion mechanism, so moving it (per-uid, or
# $TMPDIR-derived) would clear this finding by deleting the property the lock
# exists for. #1113 hardened the OPEN instead - O_NOFOLLOW against a symlink
# whose target `open(path,"w")` truncated, O_NONBLOCK + S_ISREG against a FIFO
# that hung the deploy - and accepted the finding with that reasoning recorded
# in docs/security/bandit-finding-dispositions.md.
#
# `lib/cicd/runner.py B108` was here and is CLOSED (#1113): the uv cache had no
# such contract to preserve, so it became a uid-scoped path created 0700 and
# validated for symlink, type, owner and group/other-writability before use.
finding lib/cicd/deploy/guardrails.py B108 1 #1113

# B310 - CLOSED, both sites (#1113). They carried `# noqa: S310` and a comment
# asserting a fixed https host; the comment is now code. A scheme check alone
# would NOT have cleared these - B310 is a call blacklist with no dataflow - so
# what closed them is that neither site calls `urllib.request.urlopen` any
# more: both use an OpenerDirector registering only https handlers, which
# cannot open `file:` or `ftp:` at all, plus an initial-URL check and a
# redirect-target check (urllib's default handler permits an https -> http
# downgrade). See the register's B310 section before reverting any of it.

# B314 - xml.etree parse. The XML is a JUnit report this repository itself just
# produced, so the untrusted-input premise does not hold here; #1113 records the
# provenance rather than adding a defusedxml dependency for it.
finding scripts/pytest-parallel-differential.py B314 1 #1113

